u} = 11516 	ext{ cm}^{-1}$, we can find the wavelength as:<br><br>$\lambda = \dfrac{1}{11516} 	ext{ cm}$<br><br>To convert the wavelength from cm to nm, we multiply by $10^7$ (since $1 	ext{ cm} = 10^7 	ext{ nm}$):<br><br>$\lambda = \dfrac{10^7}{11516} 	ext{ nm}$<br><br>$\lambda \approx 868.35 	ext{ nm}$<br><br>Rounding to the nearest integer, we get $868 	ext{ nm}$.<br><br>Answer: 868 nm

The wave number $ ar{
u}$ for a transition in a hydrogen atom is given by Rydberg's formula:<br><br>$ ar{
u} = \mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{H}} \left( \dfrac{1}{n_1^2} - \dfrac{1}{n_2^2} ight)$<br><br>For the Balmer series, the lower energy level is $n_1 = 2$.<br><br>The lowest transition corresponds to the minimum energy difference, which occurs for the transition from the immediately higher energy level, $n_2 = 3$.<br><br>Substituting these values into the formula:<br><br>$ ar{
u} = \mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{H}} \left( \dfrac{1}{2^2} - \dfrac{1}{3^2} ight)$<br><br>$ ar{
u} = \mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{H}} \left( \dfrac{1}{4} - \dfrac{1}{9} ight)$<br><br>$ ar{
u} = \mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{H}} \left( \dfrac{9 - 4}{36} ight)$<br><br>$ ar{
u} = \mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{H}} \left( \dfrac{5}{36} ight)$<br><br>Answer: $\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{H}}\left(\dfrac{5}{36}ight)$

